[verify] 2026-09-22 05:40 KST -- INDEPENDENT verification of code/gmm_em_gpu.py
         by a second engineer (code/verify_gpu_port.py).  Two defects found and fixed.
================================================================================

DEFECT 1 (BLOCKER, fixed) -- code/fit_gpu.py wrote to the WRONG DIRECTORY.
  fit_gpu.py never called runner._init(tag), so runner.TAG stayed "" and arms.D2_FITS kept its
  import-time default.  Measured: arms.fit_path("D2","S2",8,"kron",512,160000) resolved to
      results/gmm_fits_D2/fit_S2_Nr8_kronK512_n160000.npz        (WRONG)
  instead of
      results/gmm_fits_D2_n16e4/fit_S2_Nr8_kronK512_n160000.npz  (where the CPU run, --tag n16e4, writes)
  results/gmm_fits_D2 is the resolution target of FOUR tags: untagged, B1e4, C and supp
  (gmm_fits_D2_B1e4 -> gmm_fits_D2, gmm_fits_D2_C -> gmm_fits_D2, gmm_fits_D2_supp -> gmm_fits_D2).
  arms.load_fits() takes whatever .npz files exist and only raises if it finds NONE, so a
  --tag C / --tag B1e4 analysis at ntrain=160000 would have found ONLY these 3-4 GPU-produced fits
  and selected b* from that subset, silently.  That is precisely the "two fitting codes in one
  comparison" failure the equivalence test exists to prevent -- arriving through the file routing
  rather than through the arithmetic.
  FIX: fit_gpu.py takes <tag> as a REQUIRED 5th argument, calls _init(tag) first, and asserts
  os.path.dirname(out) == d_fits_d2().  launch_fit_gpu.sh passes TAG=n16e4.  A 4-argument
  invocation now exits with a usage message instead of writing to the wrong place.

DEFECT 2 (real, fixed) -- best-validation snapshot aliased the live tensor.
  gmm_em_gpu.py used covs.cpu().numpy() for best["covs"].  t.cpu() is a NO-OP when t is already on
  the CPU, so .numpy() returned a VIEW sharing memory with the live covs tensor; every later
  in-place covs[k] = ... overwrote the snapshot.  t2_gmm.fit_gmm_em uses covs.copy(), a real copy.
  Effect on device="cpu": early stopping silently returns the FINAL-iteration covariances paired
  with the best-validation pi.  Measured before the fix, kron n=400 K=64: d_cov = 2.79 (relative),
  i.e. a completely different model, while ll_val and pi matched exactly -- so it is invisible to
  any check that looks only at likelihoods.
  Effect on device="cuda": NONE.  .cpu() on a CUDA tensor always copies.  Confirmed empirically,
  not just by argument: in all three of the author's saved CPU/GPU pairs it_best < n_iter
  (90<131, 80<86, 90<131), so the snapshot is taken well before the end, and the GPU covs still
  agree with numpy's to 5.2e-14..7.9e-13.  Had the alias bitten on CUDA the disagreement would be
  O(1).  => the four fits currently running on GPUs 2-5 are NOT affected by this fix.
  FIX: _host(t) = np.array(t.detach().cpu()), used for every host hand-off.

NOT DEFECTS, but differences worth recording:
  * fit_task's per-run `sec` starts before _sets(); fit_gpu.py's starts after.  Measured
    sec_data = 0.6-0.8 s, i.e. <= 0.15% of any quoted time.  Immaterial.
  * scipy.special.logsumexp vs torch.logsumexp: both max-shifted, agree to round-off.
  * selection tie-break: max() over dict insertion order.  fit_gpu inserts sorted (kappa,restart);
    cmd_fit inserts in pool-completion order.  Only reachable on an exact ll_val tie; the smallest
    real margin in this grid is 4.9e-3 nat (see below).  Pre-existing in cmd_fit either way.
  * fit_gpu.py does not call write_fit_table(); results/gmm_fit_D2_n16e4.txt comes from the CPU run.
  * cuBLAS is not pinned deterministic, so GPU fits are not guaranteed bitwise reproducible
    run-to-run the way the CPU fits are.  Within the round-off budget below, but worth knowing.

EVERYTHING ELSE IN THE EM MATCHES t2_gmm.py:136-187 expression by expression -- seed init and
rng.choice; seed_cov (vdot conjugation convention); _loglik via Cholesky + explicit inv(L),
logdet = 2*sum(log diag L), -N log(pi); the it>=20 gain test; validation every val_every with
`v > best` / `it - best >= patience`, snapshot taken BEFORE the M-step; the kappa MAP shrinkage;
the floor*cbar*I floor; the kron 3-sweep flip-flop INCLUDING the tr(T)=Nt-then-add-floor ordering
and R rescaled by the pre-floor c; the nk<N / nk<4 re-seed thresholds, the nk[k]=max(nk[k],1.0)
mutation that feeds logpi, Tk[k]=I, and the rng.integers(n) draw order; the returned dict.
The three "oddities" the author flagged are real in the read-only file and faithfully reproduced.

